  "fashion": {
    "Overview": "New Year themed Ming-style Hanfu look, with jiaoling top, bijia with faux white fur trim, and Mamian skirt with faux woven gold Most merchants will launch Spring Festival-themed clothing every year. Chinese clothing is often worn at this time, such as with fancier Qipao, Tangzhuang, and the rising Hanfu. Traditional Chinese Spring Festival symbolism is the most important aspect of the fashion. Silk is seen in most traditional Chinese aesthetic. As the festival is at the turn of spring and winter, warmth is another main motif. Woven wool, down feathers, faux fur, and thick cotton are seen. This also makes the Ming-style Hanfu a widely accepted part of the aesthetic. Wear clothes that are…",
    "Tops": "Guochao printed T-shirt",
    "Bottoms": "source: North California Hanfu Association",
    "Dresses": "Silk Qipao or Qipao -styled dress",
    "Outerwear": "Red down jacket; Neo-Chinese or Han-yuansu overcoat; Cotton-padded jacket for kids; Short Ming-style Bijia (比甲) trimmed with white fur; Ming-style fur coat or long jacket",
    "Shoes": "Guochao sneakers",
    "Accessories": "Red string bracelets; Jade bracelets; Hair accessory with fake braids or buns, especially for kids; handheld lanterns, especially for kids; White headwear is forbidden",
    "Hair and Makeup": "Updo; Braid or braids, with red ribbon braided into one of them; Buns; Anything aggresive or rebellious is avoided; Unnatural hair color is avoided"
  },
